2026-09-16T13:57:18.744616Z [] {'tender_id': None, 'source_reference_id': None, 'source_id': None, 'title': None, 'issuing_agency': None, 'category': None, 'status': None, 'publish_date': None, 'closing_date': None, 'value_amount': None, 'value_currency': None, 'value_notes': None, 'location': None, 'description': 'No source documents were provided, therefore no tender details can be extracted or summarised. Please provide relevant source documents to generate a summary.', 'contact_name': None, 'contact_email': None, 'contact_phone': None, 'lodgment_address': None, 'documents': [{'file_name': 'Addendum - 1.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n      \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 01 ? PROC0018432 \n1 \n      \nAddendum - 01 \nThe National Indigenous Australians Agency (the Agency) will publish general information and all questions of substance and \nanswers affecting this Request for Tender on AusTender.  \nIn all other respects the RFT remains the same as published on AusTender.  \nDetails \nDetail  \nDetail \nReference number: \nPROC0018432 \nDescription: \nIndigenous Business Verification and Support Services \nAddendum number: \n01 \nDate of addendum: \n18 August 2026 \n \nGeneral Information \n1. The deadline for registrations to complete the required registration documentation and to attend the \nIndustry Briefing has been extended from 2:00pm Tuesday 18 August 2026 to 5:00pm Wednesday 19 \nAugust 2026 (AEST). \n2. For all Tenderers who have completed the required registrations, further information (Statement of \nRequirement Appendices 7-10) will be available from the Data Room on Friday 21 August 2026. \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'Addendum - 2.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \nOFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 02 |PROC0018432 \n1 \nOFFICIAL \nAddendum 02 \nThe National Indigenous Australians Agency (the Agency) will publish general advice and all questions of substance and \nanswers affecting this Request for Tender on AusTender.   \nIn all other respects the RFT, and related Addendums issued, remains the same as published on AusTender.   \nDetails \nDetail  \nDetail \nReference number: \nPROC0018432 \nDescription: \nIndigenous Business Verification and Support Services \nAddendum number: \n02 \nDate of addendum: \n25 August 2026 \n \nGeneral Advice \nIndustry Briefing: Held Thursday 20 August 2026.  The questions below (1 - 8) were asked at the Industry Briefing.  \nQuestions and responses have been grouped by subject.   \nData Room: The following are now available through the Data Room. \n? \nRecording of the Industry Briefing; \n? \nPowerPoint presentation from the Industry Briefing; and \n? \nStatement of Requirement Appendices 7 ? 10. \nAccess has been provided to the Data Room to those who have registered (refer Request for Tender, Clause 9). \n\nOFFICIAL \n \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 02 ? PROC0018432 \n2  \nQuestions and Answers \n \nQuestion 1:  \nIs NIAA seeking a single prime provider to deliver the entire service, or would consortium and \npartnership models be welcomed where different parties provide verification, technology and \nnational service capability?  \nCan we use third party hosted websites or must the tender build, develop and host websites \ninternally?  \nCan a provider subcontract some parts of the tender with another business? \nAnswer 1: \nNIAA intends to enter into a contract with a single legal entity that will be responsible for the \nperformance of the contract.  Refer to Request for Tender, Clause 13 for detail on Group Tenders. \nPart tenders will not be considered.  Refer Request for Tender, Clause 12.   \nThe Provider must deliver a national service.  Refer Statement of Requirement, Clause 16.   \nThe Provider may subcontract part of the Services where the conditions of Draft Contract, Clause \n16 are met, including obtaining the NIAA?s prior consent.  Tenderers should note obligations on \nthe Provider and Subcontractor in the Draft Contract, including but not limited to: \n? \nClause 12 (Provider Warranties);  \n? \nClause 21 (NIAA Security);  \n? \nClause 22 (Protection of Personal Information); and  \n? \nClause 24 (Liability). \n \n \nQuestion 2: \nWill NIAA facilitate access to authoritative government data sources for verification, or is the \nprovider expected to independently establish those data-sharing and integration \narrangements? \nAnswer 2: \nThe Provider is responsible for facilitating access to data-sharing arrangements.  Refer to \nClause 11.5, Statement of Requirement for detail on Data Integration.  The NIAA will provide \nsupport if required (e.g.  if the Government agency requires another Government agency to \nauthorise or sponsor the Provider?s access).   \n \n \n\nOFFICIAL \n \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 02 ? PROC0018432 \n3  \nQuestion 3: \nHow does NIAA expect the provider to demonstrate independence where the provider may \nalso have a role in Indigenous business advocacy, supplier development or procurement market \nengagement?  \nBy performing verification activities, will the successful tenderer conflict themselves out of \nother opportunities stemming from the IPP? How will this be handled?  \nAs an Aboriginal business seeking funding through NIAA for proposals will this be a conflict of \ninterest if we become a successful tender? \nAnswer 3: \nVerifications must be independent and undertaken free from bias.  Refer Clause 5.12 and 5.14, \nStatement of Requirement.  This does not preclude the Provider from undertaking other \nGovernment funded activities, however the Provider is responsible for managing any potential \nconflicts of interest.  How the Provider will deliver Verifications independently, and how the \nProvider will manage conflicts of interest should be evidenced in the Governance and \nDecision-making Framework (refer Clause 6, Statement of Requirement) and the Conflict of \nInterest Policy (refer Clause 19, Statement of Requirement). \nThis procurement is for contracted services and is separate to the NIAA?s grant processes.   \n \n \nQuestion 4: \nWhat do the service offers for the website include? \nThe website directory needs to be a stand-alone and exclusive for the purpose of the contract, \nand not a part of any current directories.  When the contract is discontinued at the end of the \ncontract period, [does this mean] the website [must be] closed? \nAnswer 4: \nRefer Clause 10, Statement of Requirements and Clause 6, Draft Contract for requirements \nrelated to the Website.   \nCorrect ? the Website and domain must operate independently from other websites or domains, \nincluding any website or domain the Provider operates for another purpose (unless agreed \notherwise by the NIAA) and the Website must be closed down on the Contract End Date (refer \nClause 10.5 and 26, Statement of Requirement). \n \n \n\nOFFICIAL \n \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 02 ? PROC0018432 \n4  \nQuestion 5: \nCan NIAA clarify what it means by ?culturally-appropriate? service delivery for the purpose of \nthis procurement, and what evidence tenderers will be expected to provide to demonstrate \ncultural capability?  \nAre there any special conditions or weighting Indigenous Businessess tendering over non-\nIndigenous businesses? \nAnswer 5: \nThe Approach to Market documents do not reference ?culturally-appropriate? but do refer to \n?culturally-informed? and ?culturally-safe and informed?, the requirements for which are set out \nas follows: \n? \n?culturally-informed?, refer Governance and Decision-making Framework at Clauses 6.2 and \n6.3, Statement of Requirement; and \n? \n?culturally-safe and informed? refer Clause 15, Statement of Requirement. \nThe Provider is required to consult with the Indigenous business sector (and other stakeholders) \nin developing the ?Culturally-informed Verification Methodology? (Methodology) and brief NIAA \non the outcomes (refer Clause 5.3 and 5.5, Statement of Requirement). \nTenderers are required to provide relevant evidence in responses to Criterion 1 when detailing \nthe proposed approach in sub-criteria relating to developing the Methodology; delivering \n?culturally-safe and informed services?; and ensuring First Nations people are involved in the \ndelivery of the Services.   \nThere are no specific special conditions on or weighting for tenders from Indigenous businesses, \nhowever the Statement of Requirement, Clause 6.2 and 6.3 require that the Provider: \n? \nestablish a formal, Indigenous-controlled governance mechanism or ensure that First Nations \npeople are represented in the Provider?s organisational leadership; \n? \ncontinuously demonstrate that First Nations people have a determinative role in the \ngovernance of the Methodology and decision-making process; and \n? \nregularly engage with the Indigenous business sector. \n \n \n \n\nOFFICIAL \n \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 02 ? PROC0018432 \n5  \nQuestion 6: \nHas there been consultation with Supply Nation (the incumbent) regarding the overlap \nbetween this verification service and the services provided by them already? \nHow does NIAA see the future relationship between the new register, Supply Nation, ORIC and \nIndigenous chambers etc?  \nAnswer 6: \nPrior to the Approach to Market being released, the NIAA extended the contract for the \nincumbent provider (Supply Nation) to continue service delivery until 30 June 2027.  Under this \ncontract: \n? \nuntil the data Verifications commence under this procurement: The incumbent provider will \ncontinue to verify Indigenous businesses against the original Indigenous Procurement Policy \n(IPP) eligibility criteria and maintain existing ancillary services under the original criteria. \n? \nfrom the date Verifications commence under this procurement: The incumbent provider will \ncease verifying Indigenous businesses, but will maintain other ancillary services until 30 June \n2027.   \nCommunication by NIAA with the incumbent service provider in relation to the current \ncontracted services is solely being contained to business-as-usual requirements for contracted \nservices and is being managed in strict accordance with the NIAA?s Probity Plan established for \nthis RFT.   \nThe objective of this procurement is delivering the Indigenous Business Verification and \nSupporting Services which will verify eligibility against the Australian Government?s strengthened \nIPP eligibility criteria.  Verification requirements for the Office of the Registrar of Indigenous \nCorporations (ORIC) and state/territory-based procurement policies are outside the scope of this \nRFT.                                                                                                                                                                                          \n \n \nQuestion 7: \nIs the objective ultimately to reduce the need for Indigenous businesses to undergo multiple \nverification processes across jurisdictions? Right now, we have multiple state-based registers \nverifying entities too. \nAnswer 7: \nThe objective of this procurement is delivering the Indigenous Business Verification and \nSupporting Services which will verify eligibility against the Australian Government?s strengthened \nIPP eligibility criteria.  The availability of alternate verification services or other State/Territory \ngovernment verification requirements is outside the scope of this RFT.   \n \n \n\nOFFICIAL \n \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 02 ? PROC0018432 \n6  \nQuestion 8: \nIs there an ability to extend the closing time for the Request for Tender? \nAnswer 8: \nTenders must be submitted by the Closing Time as published on AusTender.   \nThe NIAA will consider requests for extension in line with Request for Tender, Clause 3 and the \nCommonwealth Procurement Rules.  Requests should be in writing, emailed to \nIPPtenders@niaa.gov.au no later than the Enquiry Cut Off Time and should set out the additional \ntime required and the purpose the additional time is required for.  If a request is submitted, \nTenderers should not assume that the request will be approved but must rely on the Close Data \nand Time as published on AusTender.  If an extension is granted it will apply to all Tenderers and \nwill be published via addenda on AusTender. \n \n \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'Addendum - 3.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \nOFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 03 |PROC0018432 \n \nOFFICIAL \nAddendum 03 \nThe National Indigenous Australians Agency (the Agency) will publish general advice and all questions of substance and \nanswers affecting this Request for Tender on AusTender.  \nIn all other respects the RFT remains the same as published on AusTender.  \nDetails \nDetail  \nDetail \nReference number: \nPROC0018432  \nDescription: \nIndigenous Business Verification and Support Services \nAddendum number: \n03 \nDate of addendum: \n11 September 2026 \nGeneral Information \nThe Agency reminds Tenderers of the following upcoming procurement milestone: \n? \nTender Closing Time: 2:00pm (AEST), Friday 18 September 2026. \n \nTenderers are encouraged to carefully review the Request for Tender, all supporting documentation and issued \naddenda when preparing their Tender response. \n \nTenderers are reminded to review the Conditions for Participation and Minimum Content and Format \nRequirements set out in the Request for Tender and ensure all required information and supporting \ndocumentation is included in their Tender submission. The NIAA will exclude a Tender from further consideration \nif the NIAA determines that the Tender does not meet any one or more of the Conditions for Participation or the \nMinimum Content and Format Requirements. \n \nTenderers are encouraged to commence lodgement of their Tender response well in advance of the Tender \nClosing Time and not rely on successfully uploading submission documents immediately prior to the closing \ndeadline. As per Request for Tender clause 4.20 Late Tenders will not be accepted unless the delay in lodging the \nTender is as a consequence of mishandling by the NIAA. \n \nAll questions received prior to the Enquiry Cut Off Time will be responded to via upcoming addenda. \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'Addendum - 4.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \nOFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 04 |PROC0018432 \n1 \nOFFICIAL \nAddendum 04 \nThe National Indigenous Australians Agency (the Agency) will publish general advice and all questions of substance and \nanswers affecting this Request for Tender on AusTender.  \nIn all other respects the RFT remains the same as published on AusTender.  \nDetails \nDetail  \nDetail \nReference number: \nPROC0018432 \nDescription: \nIndigenous Business Verification and Support Services \nAddendum number: \n04 \nDate of addendum: \n14 September 2026 \nQuestions and Answers \nQuestion 1: \nHow long must applicant data and documents be kept in the live environment, and what is the \nprocess for archiving records off the live system once no longer required? \nAnswer 1: \nTenderers should supply their own compliance approach with respect to records keeping.  \nThe Statement of Requirement (SOR) requires the Provider to securely capture, collect and \nprocess information in accordance with privacy obligations (Clause 5.14(c)), undertake a Privacy \nImpact Assessment (Clause 5.15), comply with the Privacy Act 1988 and Australian Privacy \nPrinciples (Clause 11.26), and implement data minimisation, retention limits and secure \ndisposal/de-identification (Clause 11.28). \nThe RFT also notes compliance with record keeping obligations under the Archives Act 1983 and \nCommonwealth record-keeping requirements. (RFT Clause 31.1(d)-(e)). Further information may \nbe obtained in the SOR at: \n? \nClause 5.14(c) - secure information processing \n? \nClause 5.15 - Privacy Impact Assessment \n? \nClause 11.24-11.28 - Privacy Act compliance, retention limits, secure disposal/de-\nidentification \n? \nClauses 24-26 - Transition Out and closure requirements \nRFT at: \n? \nClause 31.1(d)-(e) - record keeping and Archives Act obligations \n\nOFFICIAL \n \n \n \n OFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 04 |PROC0018432 \n2  \nQuestion 2:  \nIf the Provider engages an IT delivery subcontractor, does the subcontractor need to \nindependently attest to ASD Essential 8 Maturity Level 2, or does the prime contract attestation \ncover the whole supply chain under PSPF Policy 6 (third party risk)? \nAnswer 2: \nThe draft Contract requires Subcontractors performing Services to comply with the same \ncontractual obligations applicable to the Provider (refer draft Contract, clause 16). \nThe Provider is responsible for ensuring that all Subcontractor/s comply with PSPF Policy 6 (Third \nParty Risk Management) and ASD Essential Eight Maturity Level 1, with a preference for Level 2 \nfor specified controls and provision of maturity evidence. \nFor further information, please refer to the SOR and the draft Contract at: \nSOR \n? \nClause 11.21 - PSPF Policy 6 \n? \nClauses 11.22-11.23 - ISM and Essential Eight requirements \ndraft Contract \n? \nClause 16 - Subcontractors \n? \nClause 21 ? NIAA Security, particularly clause 21.1(a)  \n \nQuestion 3: \nIs integration with a government identity provider (e.g. myGovID) expected for applicant \nauthentication, given this is a privately delivered service rather than a government system \ndirectly? \nAnswer 3: \nNo. There is no requirement for integration with myGovID or other government identity \nproviders. For further information please refer to the SOR at: \nSOR \n? \nClause 10.5 - authenticated pages \n? \nClauses 11.21-11.23 - security requirements \n \nQuestion 4: \nConfirm which application form fields are mandatory versus optional, and which fields (e.g. \ngender categories) need to remain admin-editable post go-live without a developer engagement. \nAnswer 4: \nThe Provider is required to develop and implement an Application and Assessment Process that \ncollects all information necessary to assess applications against the approved Methodology and \nstrengthened IPP eligibility criteria, while complying with privacy, security and usability \nrequirements.  \nThe SOR intentionally specifies the information outcomes required to support verification, \nassessment, governance, compliance and reporting activities, rather than prescribing a detailed \napplication form design or field-level data model. This is left for the market to propose.  \nThe SOR specifies information and assessment requirements for verification activities (Clause \n5.12). The Provider is responsible for developing a Culturally-Informed Verification Methodology \n\nOFFICIAL \n \n \n \n OFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 04 |PROC0018432 \n3  \n(Clause 5), this includes identifying the required fields to meet the requirements in a manner \nconsistent with the SOR. \n \n \nQuestion 5: \nIs tamper-evident audit logging required on verification documents as they move through the \nreview chain, to confirm information hasn\'t been altered during processing? \nAnswer 5: \nYes, from an auditability and traceability point of view, the proposed systems should have \nauditing of who has changed what and when.  \nIn the SOR, Clause 11.35 requires centralised logging for authentication events, administrative \nactions and data changes, with logs protected in accordance with ISM guidance. Clauses 5.12 and \n5.14 also establish assessment, decision-making and audit-related requirements.  \n \nQuestion 6: \nWill the domain name need to be acquired? \nAnswer 6: \nYes. The Provider is responsible for developing, implementing and maintaining the Website (SOR \nclause 10.1). This includes the acquisition of the domain. Tenderers are advised to refer to SOR, \nClause 10 and the draft Contract for further information. \n \nQuestion 7: \nAre any/all subcontractors required to complete a deed for the tender if so, are they to be \nsubmitted with the RFT before the due date? \nAnswer 7: \nNo. Subcontractors are not required to complete the Tenderers Deed (Attachment B, Annexure 2) \nunless they are part of a Tendering Group.  \nTenderers are responsible for identifying any members of a Tendering Group, as defined in RFT \nPart 2, clause 13, Group Tenders. Further information may be obtained in the RFT at Clause 14 - \nSubcontract arrangements. \n \nQuestion 8: \nWhat is the expected SLA/turnaround time for a "Simple" versus "Complex" Application? Clause \n5.2(d) references this distinction but does not define processing timeframes, and these appear to \nsit in Appendix 5 (Performance Expectations), which is not included in this release. e.g. Workflows \nthat flag and issue with or without an escalation? \n \nAnswer 8: \nThese design elements are intentionally left to the market to propose as part of the Provider\'s \nsolution, provided the solution can meet the agreed Performance Expectations and governance \nrequirements.  \n \nThe SOR distinguishes between Simple Applications and Complex Applications and requires the \nProvider to develop and implement processes to identify and manage those categories as part of \nthe Verification process. Specifically, the Provider must undertake a preliminary analysis to \ndetermine whether an application is complex and develop processes to identify whether an \napplication is a Simple Application or a Complex Application.  Tenderers are referred to Appendix \n5 and Appendix 6 of the SOR. \n \n\nOFFICIAL \n \n \n \n OFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 04 |PROC0018432 \n4  \n \nQuestion 9: \nIs a maker-checker (reviewer/approver) workflow expected for Verification decisions, or is a \nsingle assessor\'s decision treated as final? e.g. Single or Multi-person approver (role \nmanagement). Is this approval process just a checkbox or require a signature? \nAnswer 9: \nThe SOR requires a separation between the assessment and decision-making functions but does \nnot prescribe the number of approvers, or a signature methodology. Tenderers are referred to \nSOR Clause 5.12 for further information. \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'Addendum - 5.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \nOFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 05 |PROC0018432 \n1 \nOFFICIAL \nAddendum 05 \nThe National Indigenous Australians Agency (the Agency) will publish general advice and all questions of substance and \nanswers affecting this Request for Tender on AusTender.  \nIn all other respects the RFT, and the related Addendums issued, remains the same as published on AusTender.  \nDetails \nDetail  \nDetail \nReference number: \nPROC0018432 \nDescription: \nIndigenous Business Verification and Support Services \nAddendum number: \n05 \nDate of addendum: \n15 September 2026 \nQuestions and Answers \nQuestions are grouped into themes.  \nSecurity \nQuestion 1: \nWho bears the cost of the IRAP assessment, and does the Provider select the assessor or does \nNIAA? \nAnswer 1: \nThe requirement to undertake an Infosec Registered Assessors Program (IRAP) assessment is a \nProvider obligation.  \nThe Statement of Requirement requires the Provider to "undertake an Information Security \nRegistered Assessors Program (IRAP) assessment within four months of the Commencement Date \nand every two years thereafter." (Clause 11.9(f)).  \nTenderers should propose a compliant approach to meeting this requirement as part of their \nresponse. The SOR provides that the Provider is responsible for website security and associated \ninformation technology requirements (Clauses 10.2(a)-(f), 22.2). Tenderers are referred to the \nSOR and draft contract for further information.  \nNIAA will provide no role in the selection of an Assessor. \nQuestion 2:  \nIs there a defined incident response and breach notification obligation and timeframe (for \nexample, Notifiable Data Breaches scheme reporting to NIAA)? None is specified alongside the \nPSPF/ISM/Essential Eight requirements. \n\nOFFICIAL \n \n \n \n OFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 05 |PROC0018432 \n2  \nAnswer 2: \nNo specific incident response or breach notification timeframe has been prescribed in the \nStatement of Requirement.  \nThe Provider is required to comply with applicable legislative and policy obligations, including the \nPrivacy Act 1988, Australian Privacy Principles and relevant security requirements.  \nRelevant requirements in the SOR include: \n? \ncomply with PSPF Policy 6 and Policy 10 (Clause 11.7(a)); \n? \ncomply with the ASD Information Security Manual (ISM) (Clause 11.7(b)); \n? \nmaintain Essential Eight maturity requirements (Clause 11.7(c)); and \n? \ncomply with the Privacy Act 1988 and Australian Privacy Principles (Clause 11.8 (a)).  \nThe Provider must also implement security controls including centralised logging, vulnerability \nremediation and security hardening measures. (Clauses 11.9(a)-(i)). \nTenderers should propose a compliant approach that meets these requirements and any other \napplicable legislative obligations. \n \nQuestion 3: \nWho owns encryption key management for data at rest - is this a Provider decision, or does NIAA \nmandate specific key custody arrangements? \nAnswer 3: \nEncryption key management forms part of the Provider?s proposed solution provided the solution \ncomplies with the security, privacy and information management requirements set out in the \nStatement of Requirement and the draft Contract. \nRelevant requirements in the SOR include: \n? \ncomply with the Protective Security Policy Framework (PSPF) (Clause 11.7(a)); \n? \ncomply with the ASD Information Security Manual (ISM) (Clause 11.7(b)); \n? \nhost the environment in Australia and within an ISM-aligned security framework (Clause \n11.9(a)); and \n? \ncomply with Privacy Act 1988 and Australian Privacy Principles (Clause 11.8(a)). \n \nSolution Design, Infrastructure and Reporting \nQuestion 4: \nWhat is the expected data volume and scale (applications per month, average and maximum \nattachment/document sizes, total records) to allow appropriate sizing of storage and \ninfrastructure? Verification volumes are given, but document sizes are not. \nAnswer 4: \nThe Statement of Requirement provides information regarding expected verification volumes and \nservice demand. \n\nOFFICIAL \n \n \n \n OFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 05 |PROC0018432 \n3  \nThe Statement of Requirement does not prescribe average or maximum attachment sizes, \ndocument sizes, storage volumes or infrastructure specifications. \nTenderers should propose a solution that demonstrates sufficient capacity, scalability and \nperformance to meet the requirements of the Services. \nTenderers are referred to the Statement of Requirement and associated procurement \ndocumentation when developing their proposed solution. \n \nQuestion 5: \nClause 11.12(b) requires Registry backups stored offsite in Australia for at least three months, on \na weekly basis. Is there a defined backup frequency for other data - application records, logs, \naudit and compliance records - beyond the Registry itself? \nAnswer 5: \nNo additional backup frequency has been prescribed beyond the requirements set out in the \nStatement of Requirement. \nThe Statement of Requirement requires the Provider to maintain Registry backups in accordance \nwith Clause 11.12 and to implement appropriate security, availability, business continuity and \ndisaster recovery measures. \nTenderers should propose a solution that demonstrates how these requirements will be met, \nincluding their approach to the management, protection and recovery of application records, \nlogs, audit records and compliance records. \nTenderers are referred to Clauses 11.9 to 11.12 of the Statement of Requirement for further \ninformation. \n \nQuestion 6: \nIs there a required Recovery Point Objective (RPO) and Recovery Time Objective (RTO) for \ndisaster recovery? The Business Continuity/Disaster Recovery clause does not specify recovery \ntime targets. \nAnswer 6: \nNo specific Recovery Point Objective (RPO) or Recovery Time Objective (RTO) has been prescribed \nin the Statement of Requirement. \nThe Provider is responsible for developing and implementing a Business Continuity and Disaster \nRecovery approach that supports the delivery of the Services and complies with the security, \navailability and continuity requirements set out in the Statement of Requirement. \nTenderers should propose a solution that demonstrates how these requirements will be met, \nincluding their approach to business continuity, disaster recovery, backup and recovery \narrangements. \nFor further information, tenderers should refer to Clauses 11.9 to 11.12 of the Statement of \nRequirement. \n \n\nOFFICIAL \n \n \n \n OFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 05 |PROC0018432 \n4  \nQuestion 7: \nDoes NIAA require real-time or API-based reporting access to Registry and Verification data, or \nare periodic exports (for example, monthly reports in a standard format) sufficient to meet the \nreporting obligation? \nAnswer 7: \nThe Statement of Requirement does not prescribe whether reporting access must be provided \nthrough real-time access, APIs, periodic exports or another reporting mechanism. \nTenderers should propose a solution that demonstrates how the reporting and information \nrequirements of the Services will be met. \n \nQuestion 8: \nDoes the solution require that it generate research reports and policy briefs?  \nAnswer 8: \nNo. The Statement of Requirement sets out the reporting, registry, website and information \nrequirements for the Services. Tenderers should propose a solution that demonstrates how those \nrequirements will be met. \n \nQuestion 9: \nFor the three required API integrations (IPP Reporting Solution and the Department of Finance\'s \nAusTender/GovPanels/Supplier Portal), are technical specifications (authentication method, data \nschema, rate limits) available now, or will these be negotiated during the Establishment Stage? \nAnswer 9: \nThe Statement of Requirement requires integration with identified systems as part of the \nServices. \nDetailed technical specifications, including matters such as authentication methods, data \nschemas and related implementation requirements will be addressed during the Establishment \nStage, as required. \nTenderers should propose a solution that demonstrates the capability to integrate with the \nsystems identified in the Statement of Requirement and associated procurement documentation. \n \nPricing and Commercial: \nQuestion 10: \nSOR Clause 22.2(d) places software and licensing costs under the Operational ? IT charge \ncategory, funded by the Provider. Does this extend to any Commonwealth-provided or NIAA-\nmandated software or platforms, or is all software selection and cost entirely at the Provider\'s \ndiscretion? \nAnswer 10: \nThe SOR does not specify any NIAA-mandated software or platforms. The Provider is responsible \nfor identifying, detailing and costing all components within their response. \nTenderers should refer to Clause 22.2 and the Pricing Schedule when developing their proposed \npricing. Operational information technology costs, including software, licensing and associated \nsupport services required to deliver the proposed solution, should be addressed in the Tenderer\'s \nresponse. \n\nOFFICIAL \n \n \n \n OFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 05 |PROC0018432 \n5  \nQuestion 11: \nIf the use of AI technologies is approved under clause 11.2, are the associated licensing costs to  \n \n \nbe included within Operational ? IT, or treated as a separate charge category requiring NIAA  \n \n \nagreement? \nAnswer 11: \nThe Statement of Requirement permits the use of Artificial Intelligence subject to the \nrequirements set out in Clause 11.2. \nTenderers should propose and price a solution that meets the requirements of the Services, \nincluding any proposed use of Artificial Intelligence technologies. Any costs associated with the \nTenderer\'s proposed solution should be addressed in the Tenderer\'s response and pricing \nproposal. \n \nAccess and Security Management: \nQuestion 12: \nWhat role-based access levels are required for the Registry, Website and any back-office system, \nand who is responsible for provisioning, reviewing and deprovisioning user access? The SOR \nrequires MFA for admin access but does not define a role structure. \nAnswer 12: \nThe Statement of Requirement sets out the security authentication and access control outcomes \nrequired by NIAA but does not prescribe a specific role structure or user access model. \nThe Provider is responsible for proposing an appropriate access control framework that \ndemonstrates how access will be provisioned, managed, reviewed and removed consistent with \nthe requirements of the Statement of Requirement. \nTenderers are referred to the security, privacy and ICT requirements contained within the \nStatement of Requirement, including requirements relating to authentication, access controls, \nlogging and auditability. \n \nQuestion 13: \nDoes NIAA require its own elevated access tier separate from general "Commonwealth Official" \npublic users, and if so, what functions does it need (for example, audit visibility, bulk export, \noverride of Provider decisions)? \nAnswer 13: \nThe Statement of Requirement sets out the functional, governance, compliance, reporting and \naudit outcomes required by NIAA but does not prescribe a specific user role structure or access \ntier model. \nTenderers should propose a solution that demonstrates how authorised users will be able to \nperform the functions required under the Services in accordance with the Statement of \nRequirement. This may include a higher degree of functionality and access for nominated NIAA \npersonnel, beyond those that are available to general Commonwealth Officials. \nTenderers are referred to the Registry, Website, governance, reporting and security requirements \ncontained within the Statement of Requirement when developing their proposed solution. \n  \n\nOFFICIAL \n \n \n \n OFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 05 |PROC0018432 \n6  \nQuestion 14: \nWho is responsible for commissioning penetration testing of the Website and Registry - the \nProvider or NIAA - and what frequency is required? Neither document specifies a testing cadence \nor responsible party beyond the four-month/two-yearly IRAP assessment cycle. \nAnswer 14: \nThe provider is responsible for the commissioning of penetration testing. The SOR does not \nprescribe a specific penetration testing frequency. Tenderers should propose a compliant \napproach that demonstrates how the security requirements of the Services will be met. \n \n---end of Addendum--- \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'Addendum - 6.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \nOFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 06 |PROC0018432 \n1 \nOFFICIAL \nAddendum 06 \nThe National Indigenous Australians Agency (the Agency) will publish general advice and all questions of substance and \nanswers affecting this Request for Tender on AusTender.  \nIn all other respects the RFT, and related Addenda issued, remains the same as published on AusTender.  \nDetails \nDetail  \nDetail \nReference number: \nPROC0018432 \nDescription: \nIndigenous Business Verification and Support Services \nAddendum number: \n06 \nDate of addendum: \n16 September 2026 \nGeneral Information \nThe NIAA have given due consideration to a change in the Closing Time. The NIAA acknowledges that: \n? \nThis Addendum 6 is the final addendum proposed to be issued for the RFT, given the close proximity to \nthe listed RFT Closing Time of 2:00pm AEST on Friday 18 September 2026; and \n? \nThis Addendum 6 does not provide new information that is not already provided for in detail within the \nRFT documentation.  \nIn line with the above, there will be no change to the Closing Time. Tenderers are advised to ensure they have \ncompleted the uploading of their submissions to AusTender in line with the RFT and related Addenda by the \nClosing Time. \nQuestions and Answers \nQuestion 1: \nSOR Clause 11.8(a)(ii) requires data minimisation and retention limits for Personally Identifiable  \n \n \nInformation. What are the specific retention periods for Applications, supporting documents, and \n \n \nDeregistered entity records?  \nAnswer 1: \nThe Provider is required to comply with applicable legislative and policy obligations, including the \n \n \nPrivacy Act 1988 and Australian Privacy Principles, Archives Act 1983 and Commonwealth record-\n \n \nkeeping requirements. \nClause 11.8(a) requires the Provider to implement:  \n\nOFFICIAL \n \n \n \n OFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 06 |PROC0018432 \n2  \nI. \ntechnical and organisational security controls (APP 11); \nII. \ndata minimisation and retention limits; and \nIII. \nsecure disposal or de-identification. \nThe SOR provides that, on contract expiry or termination, the Provider must provide the NIAA \nwith a copy of all publicly available data on the Registry in a format agreed with the NIAA (Clause \n25.1(a)).  \nTenderers are responsible for ensuring their proposed approach complies with all requirements \nof the draft Contract, including clause 35.4. Information relevant to Commonwealth records \nmanagement obligations, including under the Archives Act 1983, is available in General Records \nAuthority 40.  \nQuestion 2: \nDeregistered entity records must be retained and displayed for 12 months post-deregistration  \n \n \n(clause 25.1(a)(ii)). What is the required treatment of that data after the 12-month period -  \n \n \ndeletion, archiving, or transfer to NIAA? \nAnswer 2: \nThe Provider is required to comply with applicable legislative and policy obligations, including the \n \n \nPrivacy Act 1988, Australian Privacy Principles, Archives Act 1983 and Commonwealth record- \n \n \nkeeping requirements. \nClause 25.1 (a) of the SOR requires that, upon expiry or termination of the Contract, the Provider \nmust provide NIAA with: \n? \nthe Record (including profile) for each Verified Entity; and \n? \nthe Record (including profile) for each entity deregistered in the preceding 12 months. \nThe Registry requirements further require the permanent removal of a record from the Registry \n \nwhere a Verified Entity is deregistered (Clause 9.7(b)). \nTenderers should propose a compliant approach that demonstrates how these requirements and \nany applicable legislative obligations will be met. The agreed solution will be discussed during the \nnegotiation process with the preferred Tenderer. \n \nQuestion 3: \nIs there a defined data destruction or de-identification standard and schedule to apply once  \n \n \nretention limits expire? \nAnswer 3: \nThe Statement of Requirement does not prescribe a specific data destruction or de-identification \n \n \nstandard or schedule. \nThe Provider is required to comply with applicable legislative and policy obligations, including the \n \nPrivacy Act 1988, Australian Privacy Principles and Commonwealth record-keeping requirements. \n \nTenderers should propose a compliant approach that demonstrates how the requirements in \n \nClause 11.8(a), including data minimisation, retention limits and secure disposal or de-\n \nidentification, will be met. \n\nOFFICIAL \n \n \n \n OFFICIAL \nNIAA Procurement | Addendum 06 |PROC0018432 \n3  \n \nQuestion 4: \nAre complaint records, audit trails and spot-check evidence subject to different retention periods \n \n \nthan Verification records, and if so, what are they? \nAnswer 4: \nThe Statement of Requirement does not prescribe separate retention periods for complaints,  \n \n \naudit trails, spot checks and Verification records. \nThe SOR requires the Provider to establish and maintain records relating to: \n? \nApplications and Verifications (Clause 5.12); \n? \nComplaints and complaint handling activities (Clauses 8.1-8.5);  \n? \nCompliance activities (Clauses 7.2-7.16);  \n? \nAnnual audits (Clauses 7.18-7.20);  \n? \nIndigenous Economic Benefit Reviews (Clauses 7.21-7.23);  \n? \nSpot Checks (Clauses 7.24-7.26); and   \n? \nCentralised logging of authentication events, administrative actions and data changes (Clause \n11.9(d)).  \nThe SOR further requires that logs be retained and protected in accordance with ISM guidance \n(Clause 11.9(d)). The Provider must also comply with applicable privacy, security and record- \n keeping obligations. \nTenderers should propose a compliant approach that demonstrates how these requirements will \n \nbe met. \n \n \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'IBVSS - Attachment A - Statement of Requirement - RELEASE.txt', 'extracted_text': " \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n \n \n \n \nRequest for tender for the \nprovision of Indigenous Business \nVerification and Support Services \nReference number: PROC0018432 \n \n \nAttachment A: Statement of Requirement \n \n \n\n2 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nContents \n1. \nPurpose of SOR ......................................................................................................................... 4 \n2. \nObjectives of the procurement ................................................................................................... 4 \n3. \nBackground ................................................................................................................................ 4 \n4. \nOverview of Services .................................................................................................................. 5 \nIndigenous Business Verification ........................................................................................... 7 \n5. \nCulturally-informed Verification Methodology (Methodology) ..................................................... 7 \nThree-part test ..................................................................................................................................... 8 \nArtificial Intelligence ............................................................................................................................. 8 \nIndigenous Enterprise (Strengthened IPP Criteria).............................................................................. 8 \nIndigenous Joint Venture and Complex Business Structures (Strengthened IPP Criteria) .................. 8 \nApplication and Assessment Processes (Verifications) ....................................................................... 9 \nServices and Charges: Verifications ? Business as Usual (BAU) ...................................................... 10 \nServices and Charges: Verifications - Surge ..................................................................................... 11 \nService Cap - Verifications ................................................................................................................ 11 \n6. \nGovernance and Decision-Making Framework ........................................................................ 12 \n7. \nCompliance and Assurance Framework .................................................................................. 13 \nReferral to law enforcement and regulatory agencies ....................................................................... 14 \nCompliance: External Data Monitoring .............................................................................................. 14 \nCompliance: Verified Entities? Reporting Obligations ......................................................................... 14 \nAssurance: Annual Audits .................................................................................................................. 15 \nAssurance: Indigenous Economic Benefit Review ............................................................................ 15 \nAssurance: Spot checks .................................................................................................................... 15 \nAssurance: Services and Charges .................................................................................................... 16 \nService Cap ? Assurance Activities ................................................................................................... 16 \n8. \nComplaint Handling Framework ............................................................................................... 17 \nSolution (Registry, Website and IT) ..................................................................................... 17 \n9. \nRegistry .................................................................................................................................... 17 \n10. Website ..................................................................................................................................... 19 \n11. IT requirements ........................................................................................................................ 20 \nData Integration ................................................................................................................................. 21 \nCybersecurity: ................................................................................................................................... 21 \nSpecific controls around the hosting and Solution development ....................................................... 22 \nMaintenance of Infrastructure and Scheduled Downtime .................................................................. 22 \nCompatibility ...................................................................................................................................... 23 \nBusiness continuity and disaster recovery......................................................................................... 23 \n12. Guidance and support (including Helpdesk) ............................................................................ 23 \nGuidance ........................................................................................................................................... 23 \n\n3 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nSupport (including Helpdesk) ............................................................................................................ 24 \n13. Communication ........................................................................................................................ 24 \nCommunication Strategy ................................................................................................................... 24 \n14. Establishment Stage and Interim Services .............................................................................. 24 \nEstablishment Stage ......................................................................................................................... 24 \nProject Plans ..................................................................................................................................... 24 \nInterim Services ................................................................................................................................ 25 \n15. Culturally-safe and informed delivery ....................................................................................... 25 \n16. National service ........................................................................................................................ 25 \n17. Continuous improvement ......................................................................................................... 26 \nAnnual Survey ................................................................................................................................... 26 \nWebsite ............................................................................................................................................. 26 \nGuidance and Support ...................................................................................................................... 26 \nOrganisation requirements .................................................................................................. 26 \n18. Key Personnel .......................................................................................................................... 26 \n19. Policies ..................................................................................................................................... 27 \n20. Data and Reports ..................................................................................................................... 27 \n21. Contract governance and meetings ......................................................................................... 27 \nProvider charges ................................................................................................................. 27 \n22. Proposed charge structure ....................................................................................................... 27 \n23. Costs and charges to users...................................................................................................... 28 \nContract closure requirements (including Transition Out) .................................................... 28 \n24. Transition Out ........................................................................................................................... 28 \n25. Registry data on closure........................................................................................................... 29 \n26. Website on closure ................................................................................................................... 29 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n4 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n1. \nPurpose of SOR  \n1.1 \nThe purpose of the Statement of Requirement (SOR) is to set out: \na. \nthe services required to be delivered by a contracted service provider (Provider) under \nthe procurement, and  \nb. \nthe roles and responsibilities of the National Indigenous Australians Agency (NIAA). \n2. \nObjectives of the procurement  \n2.1 \nThe objectives of the procurement (Objectives) are to select a Provider to deliver national, \nindependent Indigenous business verification and support services (Services) for the \nimplementation of the Indigenous Procurement Policy (IPP) reforms and: \na. \nmaintain the public?s confidence in the integrity of the IPP; \nb. \nperform external, independent assessments of whether an entity (enterprise or joint \nventure) is Indigenous, and therefore eligible under the IPP, through the consistent \napplication of a Culturally-informed Verification Methodology;  \nc. \nprovide the Australian Government and the public with reliable and secure access to \naccurate and up-to-date information on verified Indigenous Enterprises and Indigenous \nJoint Ventures that are eligible under the Australian Government?s IPP eligibility criteria; \nd. \nwork collaboratively with stakeholders, including the Indigenous business sector and \nCommonwealth Agencies to ensure the Services are delivered at all times: \n(i) under robust governance, decision making, compliance and assurance frameworks;  \n(ii) through the use of fair and transparent application, assessment and complaint \nhandling processes; and \n(iii) in a manner that is culturally-safe and informed; and  \ne. \nwork collaboratively with the NIAA to: \n(i) support the Australian Government?s efforts to increase participation of genuine \nIndigenous business in Australian Government procurement; and \n(ii) support Indigenous businesses stakeholders transition from the original IPP eligibility \ncriteria to the strengthened IPP eligibility criteria. \n3. \nBackground \n3.1 \nThe IPP is the primary mechanism to drive Indigenous business participation within the \nAustralian Government Procurement Framework. The IPP was introduced in 2015 and has \ngenerated over $13.8 billion in Australian Government contracts for Indigenous Enterprises. \nThis includes over 90,000 contracts awarded to more than 4,900 Indigenous Enterprises. As \nthe sector has grown and matured, so too has the capability of Indigenous Enterprises to \nparticipate in opportunities of increasing scale and value. In 2024-25, over 1,200 Indigenous \nEnterprises secured 13,144 new contracts, which were valued in total $1.6 billion. \n3.2 \nIn February 2025, the Australian Government announced reforms to the IPP to provide more \nopportunities to Indigenous Enterprises and to advance First Nations economic empowerment. \n3.3 \nA key component of IPP reforms is strengthening the IPP eligibility criteria to ensure First \nNations business owners accessing the IPP are empowered to run their enterprises and \nexercise their rights as majority owners.  \n3.4 \nThe IPP eligibility criteria will be strengthened by:  \na. \nchanging the definition of ?Indigenous Enterprise? from 50 per cent or more Indigenous \nowned to 51 per cent or more Indigenous owned and controlled; and \nb. \nchanging the definition of ?Indigenous Joint Venture? (IJV) from 50 per cent or more \nIndigenous owned and controlled to 51 per cent or more Indigenous owned and \ncontrolled; and \n\n5 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nc. \nintroducing an Indigenous Economic Benefit Review requirement for IJVs and other \nComplex Business Structures.1  \n3.5 \nThis Request for Tender (RFT) seeks a Provider to: \na. \nverify that an entity, including businesses, joint ventures and complex business \nstructures, meet the strengthened IPP Eligibility Criteria; \nb. \nmaintain a publicly accessible and searchable Registry (supported by Website and IT) of \nVerified Entities; \nc. \nconduct compliance and assurance activities to ensure that entities genuinely continue to \nmeet the strengthened IPP eligibility criteria; and \nd. \ndeliver related services. \n3.6 \nThere will be a defined period to support stakeholders, including current Indigenous Enterprises \nunder the original IPP eligibility criteria, to transition from the original IPP eligibility criteria to the \nstrengthened IPP eligibility criteria (Transition Period). \na. \nThe Transition Period commences on 1 July 2026 and will end 12 months after the date \nVerifications commence.  \nb. \nDuring the Transition Period, businesses will be eligible for the IPP if they meet either the \noriginal IPP eligibility criteria or the strengthened IPP eligibility criteria.  \n3.7 \nAfter the Transition Period, Indigenous Enterprises will only be eligible if they meet the \nstrengthened IPP eligibility criteria. \n4. \nOverview of Services \n4.1 \nThe NIAA is seeking a Provider to deliver national, independent Indigenous business \nverification and support services (Services) for the implementation of the IPP reforms.  \n4.2 \nIn delivering the Services, the Provider must: \na. \ndevelop and implement a Culturally-informed Verification Methodology (Methodology); \nb. \ndevelop and implement an independent Application and Assessment Process \n(Verification); \nc. \ndevelop and implement a Governance and Decision-making Framework; \nd. \ndevelop and implement a Compliance and Assurance Framework Verified Entities; \ne. \ndevelop a Complaint Handling Framework and manage complaints; \nf. \ndevelop and maintain a Registry; \ng. \ndevelop and maintain a Website; \nh. \ndevelop and maintain supporting Information Technology (IT) capabilities; \ni. \nprovide Data and Reports to the NIAA; \nj. \nprovide Guidance and support; and \nk. \ndeliver communications, \nas set out in this SOR and as agreed with the NIAA. \n4.3 \nThe Provider must consistently deliver the Services in a culturally-safe and informed manner. \n4.4 \nThe Provider must deliver the Services nationally. \n4.5 \nThe Provider must deliver the Services at no cost: \na. \nto Applicants;  \n \n1 the requirement for an Indigenous Economic Benefit Review replaces requirements for (a) strategy to build the \ncapability of the Indigenous business partners and (b) an Indigenous workforce strategy.  \n\n6 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nb. \nfor Verification or registration of Verified Entities on the Registry; and  \nc. \nto external stakeholders accessing the Registry. \n4.6 \nIn establishing the Services:  \na. \nthe Provider and the NIAA will agree due dates for deliverables and milestones (refer \nAppendix 1), including:  \n(i) delivery of Interim Services (target due date is within four weeks of the \nCommencement Date); and \n(ii) commencing Verifications (target date of 12 weeks after the Commencement Date). \n4.7 \nThe Provider must: \na. \nfrom the date Verifications commence, provide additional capacity for 12 months to \naddress the anticipated higher-than-normal demand for Verification during that period; \nand \nb. \nfrom 12 months after the date Verifications commence, provide Compliance and \nAssurance Activities to ensure Verified Entities remain eligible under the Methodology. \n4.8 \nIn delivering the Services, the Provider must: \na. \ncollaborate with the NIAA to support the Indigenous business sector and Australian \nGovernment procurement officials to understand and adapt to the strengthened IPP \neligibility criteria; \nb. \ncollaborate with the NIAA, including attending meetings, providing information (data, \nreports) and advice on request; and \nc. \ncollaborate with the NIAA, the Indigenous business sector and Australian Government \nprocurement officials on continuous improvement of the Services over the life of the \nContract.  \n \nNote to Tenderers: Refer to Worksheet B in Attachment C (Pricing Schedule) for proposed timing \nof: \n? \nthe Transition Period ? starting on 1 July 2026 and ending 12 months after the date \nVerifications commence. ?Transition Period? is defined in the context of the implementation \nof the strengthened IPP eligibility criteria in general. For clarity, the obligations under the \nContract commence on the Commencement date, not 1 July 2026.  \n? \nthe Establishment Stage ? starting on the Commencement Date and ending the date on \nwhich Verifications commence.  \n? \nthe Interim Services ? starting no more than 4 weeks after the Commencement Date and \nending the day before the date Verifications commence. \n? \nSurge Period ?starting on the date Verifications commence, extending for 12 months. \nThe date Verifications commence is the date the Provider commences receiving applications for \nVerifications.  \n \n\n7 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nIndigenous Business Verification \n5. \nCulturally-informed Verification Methodology (Methodology) \nNote to Tenderers: Proposed charges for the Methodology requirements should be set out in the \nAttachment C (Pricing Schedule) at: \n? \nWorksheet 2: if associated with an identified deliverable; \n? \nWorksheet 3: for one-off, upfront charges as required during the Establishment Stage; or \n? \nWorksheet 4: for fixed, ongoing charges. \n5.1 \nThe Provider must develop and implement a Methodology to set out how the Provider will: \na. \nverify that an entity meets the definition of an Indigenous Enterprise; \nb. \nverify that an entity meets the definition of an Indigenous Joint Venture; and \nc. \nundertake Indigenous Economic Benefit reviews. \n5.2 \nThe Methodology must: \na. \nbe consistent with the IPP;2  \nb. \napply the three-part test outlined at Clause 5.8; \nc. \napply the strengthened IPP eligibility criteria for Indigenous Enterprises outlined at \nClause 5.10 and Indigenous Joint Ventures outlined at Clause 11;  \nd. \ndefine and set criteria for: \n(i) Complex Applications (relative to Simple Applications); \n(ii) Complex Business Structures; and \n(iii) any other key terms applied in the Methodology;  \ne. \nbe sufficiently clear and detailed so that it is consistently repeatable and minimises the \nneed for personal discretion or judgement; and  \nf. \nbe informed by the guidance set out in the: \n(i) Guidance on verifying 51 per cent or more Indigenous Ownership at Appendix Error! \nReference source not found.; \n(ii) Guidance on verifying 51 per cent of more Indigenous Control at Appendix Error! \nReference source not found.; and  \n(iii) Guidance on Indigenous Economic Benefit review at Appendix Error! Reference \nsource not found..  \n5.3 \nIn developing the Methodology, the Provider must consult with: \na. \nthe Indigenous business sector; \nb. \nthe NIAA; and \nc. \nAustralian Government Procurement Officials.  \n5.4 \nThe Provider must develop, seek and obtain the NIAA?s written approval of a plan, outlining \nhow it will consult with stakeholders (Consultation Plan). \n5.5 \nBefore accepting applications for Verification, the Provider must: \na. \nbrief the NIAA on the outcomes of the consultation, including on any significant issues, \nrisks, complaints or objections raised during the consultation (Consultation Brief); \nb. \nbrief the NIAA on the proposed Methodology (Methodology Brief); and \n \n2 https://www.niaa.gov.au/our-work/employment-and-economic-development/indigenous-procurement-policy-ipp  \n\n8 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nc. \nseek and obtain the NIAA?s written approval on the Methodology as it relates to \nVerification of: \n(i) Indigenous Enterprises; and \n(ii) Indigenous Joint Ventures. \n5.6 \nThe Provider must: \na. \ncontinuously monitor and, in consultation with the NIAA, identify opportunities to refine \nthe Methodology to ensure it continues to deliver on the IPP requirements; \nb. \nundertake one comprehensive review of the Methodology, including consulting with the \nstakeholders identified at Clause Error! Reference source not found. above, within \n18 months of the Commencement Date;  \nc. \nif there are any changes to the IPP during the Contract Period, collaborate with the NIAA \nto amend the Methodology as required to ensure it aligns with those changes; and  \nd. \nseek and obtain the NIAA?s written approval prior to making any significant changes it \nidentifies are required to the Methodology. \n5.7 \nThe Provider must meet with the NIAA in accordance with Contract governance and meetings \n(refer Appendix 3), to resolve issues with the Methodology or its implementation.  \n \nNote to Tenderers: For clarity, the consultation outlined above and required under the Contract \nmust only occur after contract commencement. Tenderers are not required to conduct consultation \nas part of the Approach to Market.  \nThree-part test \n5.8 \nFor a person to be considered Indigenous for the purposes of assessing Indigenous ownership \nor control, they must satisfy a three-part test. The three-part test requires that a person meet all \nthree criteria outlined below: \na. \nis of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander descent;  \nb. \nidentifies as an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person; and \nc. \nis recognised or accepted as an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person by the \ncommunity in which they live or have previously lived. \nArtificial Intelligence \n5.9 \nThe Provider must not use Artificial Intelligence:  \na. \nin the application of the Methodology as set out in Clause 5.2; \nb. \nin conducting assessments or making decisions as set out in Clause 5.12; or \nc. \nto deliver any other part of the Service without the prior written approval of the NIAA.  \nIndigenous Enterprise (Strengthened IPP Criteria) \n5.10 An Entity will be verified as an Indigenous Enterprise if it is: \na. \n51 per cent or more Indigenous owned (refer Appendix Error! Reference source not \nfound.); and \nb. \n51 per cent of more Indigenous controlled (refer Appendix Error! Reference source not \nfound.). \nIndigenous Joint Venture and Complex Business Structures (Strengthened IPP Criteria) \n5.11 An Entity will be verified as an Indigenous Joint Venture if it is: \na. \n51 per cent or more Indigenous owned (refer Appendix Error! Reference source not \nfound.);  \n\n9 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nb. \n51 per cent of more Indigenous controlled (refer Appendix Error! Reference source not \nfound.); and \nc. \nable to demonstrate Indigenous Economic Benefit (refer Appendix Error! Reference \nsource not found.), \nand meets the following criteria: \nd. \nit as an incorporated company formed through the incorporation of an Indigenous \nbusiness and a non-Indigenous business; \ne. \nit is registered in Australia;  \nf. \nit is for profit (i.e. is able to distribute its equity to its shareholders and not be a registered \ncharity in its own right);  \ng. \nit is able to trade as a business in its own right; and \nh. \nit is able to demonstrate commercial independence. \nApplication and Assessment Processes (Verifications) \nNote to Tenderers: Proposed charges for the Application and Assessment Process requirements \nshould be set out in the Attachment C (Pricing Schedule) at: \n? \nWorksheet 2: if associated with an identified deliverable; \n? \nWorksheet 3: for one-off, upfront charges as required during the Establishment Stage; \n? \nWorksheet 4: for fixed, ongoing charges; or \n? \nWorksheet 6: for demand-based charges incurred monthly in arrears during the life of the \ncontract. \n5.12 The Provider must develop and implement an independent Application and Assessment \nProcesses (Verification) to: \na. \nreceive Applications for Verification from Applicants using a consistent, repeatable \nprocess that collects all required information in a standard format; and \nb. \nreceive supporting documents in common formats (files with extensions .docx, .xlsx and \n.pdf) or other formats agreed between the Provider and the NIAA, \nand, for each Application: \nc. \nundertake a preliminary assessment of its completeness and identify any missing \ninformation; \nd. \nundertake a preliminary analysis to determine whether it is complex and, if so, determine \nwhether additional time may be required for the Verification process; \ne. \nacknowledge receipt and advise Applicants of the next steps (including requesting \nmissing information) and estimated response time; \nf. \nundertake any checks against, or collect any additional information required, from third \nparty data sources, including the Australian Business Register (ABR);  \ng. \nsubmit the Application for assessment against the Methodology to an appropriately \ntrained member of staff; \n(i) the assessment must be undertaken by a person or persons employed by the \nProvider and not incorporate or rely on the use of AI.  \nh. \nfor Complex Applications, undertake additional due diligence as required; \ni. \nsubmit the assessment to an internal Delegate for decision and record the reasons for \nthis decision; \n(i) the decision must be made by a person or persons employed by the Provider and not \nincorporate or rely on the use of AI.  \nand, if the Application is successful: \n\n10 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n(ii) notify the Applicant; and  \n(iii) arrange for the Applicant to be included on the Registry, \nor, if the Application is unsuccessful: \n(iv) notify the Applicant of the outcome and the reasons for the decision; and \n(v) ensure the Applicant is provided with procedural fairness, including allowing for \nappeal, review or the correction of information that is material to the decision.  \n5.13 The Provider must develop and implement a process to identify whether an Application is: \na. \nA Simple Application, or \nb. \nA Complex Application. \n5.14 The Provider must develop, implement and routinely review processes to: \na. \nensure the Assessment and decision processes are free from bias, including processes \nto declare and manage conflicts of interest;  \nb. \nprovide Applicants with notice on the process, including providing advice on: \n(i) the Assessment steps; \n(ii) the information collected; and  \n(iii) if and how third-party source information may be used as part of the Assessment;  \nc. \nsecurely capture, collect and process information in accordance with the Provider?s \nprivacy obligations; and \nd. \ntrack and monitor the progress of Applications from receipt through to notification of \noutcome.  \n \nNote to Tenderers: The Provider is required undertake a Privacy Impact Assessment (PIA) - refer \nD18 at Appendix 1 and clause 22.3 of Attachment D (Draft Contract). A PIA is a systematic \nassessment of a project that identifies the impact that the project might have on the privacy of \nindividuals, and sets out recommendations for managing, minimising or eliminating that impact. \nMore information is available through the Office of the Australian Information Commissioner \n(www.oaic.gov.au).  \n \n5.15 The Provider must report to the NIAA on Application processing in the Applications Report as \nspecified under Data and Reports (refer Appendix 3). \n5.16 The Provider acknowledges that: \na. \nit is solely responsible for any decision it makes as part of the Verification process (refer \nClause 5.12), including when applying the three-part test (refer Clause 5.8); and \nb. \ndecisions it makes as part of a Verification: \n(i) are made independently of the NIAA and the Commonwealth; and \n(ii) must be made without interference or influence from third parties. \nServices and Charges: Verifications ? Business as Usual (BAU) \nNote to Tenderers: Proposed charges for Verifications - Business as Usual (BAU) requirements \nshould be set out in the Attachment C (Pricing Schedule) at: \n? \nWorksheet 6: for demand-based charges incurred monthly in arrears during the life of the \ncontract. \n5.17 The Provider acknowledges that the volume of Verifications required will be determined by the \ndemand for those services up to a Service Cap (refer Clause 5.25).  \n\n11 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n5.18 The NIAA will pay the Provider each month the sum of two components: \na. \nThe number of Verifications of Indigenous Enterprises completed in the preceding month:  \n(i) Up to 100 (Rate 1), or \n(ii) Between 101 and 125 (Rate 2), or \n(iii) Between 126 and 150 (Rate 3) \nplus the Charge for each additional Verification above 150 up to the Service Cap.  \nb. \nThe number of Verifications of Indigenous Joint Ventures / Complex Business Structures \nin the preceding month: \n(i) Up to 5 per month (Rate 1), or \n(ii) Between 6 and 8 per month (Rate 2), or \n(iii) Between 9 and 10 per month (Rate 3) \nplus the Charge for each additional Verification above 10 up to the Service Cap. \n5.19 A Verification is completed if all steps outlined at Clause 5.12 are completed. \n5.20 The Provider must complete Verifications within the timeframes agreed in the performance \nexpectations set out in Appendix 5 to this SOR (Performance Expectations). \nServices and Charges: Verifications - Surge  \nNote to Tenderers: Proposed charges for Verifications - Surge requirements should be set out in \nthe Attachment C (Pricing Schedule) at: \n? \nWorksheet 7: for demand-based charges incurred monthly in arrears during the Surge \nPeriod. \n5.21 The Provider acknowledges that there will be additional demand for the 12-month period \nfollowing commencement of Verifications (Surge Period). \n5.22 The Provider must provide additional capacity for the Surge Period to respond to the additional \ndemand for Verifications. \n5.23 To cover the additional resources required to meet demand during the Surge Period, in addition \nto the Verification ? BAU, the NIAA will pay the Provider each month for 12 months the sum of \ntwo components: \na. \nThe additional number of Verifications of Indigenous Enterprises completed in the \npreceding month:  \n(i) Up to 250 (Rate 1), or \n(ii) Between 251 and 450 (Rate 2), or \n(iii) Between 451 and 600 (Rate 3) \nPlus the Charge for each additional Verification above 600.  \nb. \nThe additional number of Verifications of Indigenous Joint Ventures / Complex Business \nStructures \n(i) Up to 7 per month (Rate 1), or \n(ii) Between 8 and 11 per month (Rate 2), or \n(iii) Between 12 and 15 per month (Rate 3) \nPlus the Charge for each additional Verification above 15.  \n5.24 A Verification is completed if all steps outlined at Clauses 5.12 are completed. \n\n12 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nService Cap - Verifications \n5.25 The maximum number of Verifications that can be completed within a month will be limited to a \nService Cap. \n5.26 The NIAA will not pay for Verifications above the Service Cap unless the Provider has sought \nand obtained the NIAA?s prior written approval to increase the Service Cap. \n5.27 The Service Cap for Business as Usual (BAU) Verifications is: \na. \n160 per month for Indigenous Enterprise Verifications (i.e. 150 Verifications at Rate 3 \nplus 10 additional Verifications) and \nb. \n12 per month for Indigenous Joint Ventures / Complex Business Structures (i.e. 10 \nVerifications at Rate 3 plus 2 additional Verifications). \n5.28 During the Surge Period, the Service Cap for Verifications will be increased by: \na. \n640 per month for Indigenous Enterprise Verifications (i.e. 600 Verifications at Rate 3 \nplus 40 additional Verifications), combined with the Service Cap for BAU Verifications \ntotals 800 per month; and \nb. \n20 per month for Indigenous Joint Ventures / Complex Business Structures (i.e. 15 \nVerifications at Rate 3 plus 5 additional Verifications), combined with the Service Cap for \nBAU Verifications totals 32 per month. \n5.29 The Provider and the NIAA may agree to change the Service Cap in writing. The NIAA will not \nunreasonably refuse a request from the Provider to increase the Service Cap. \n5.30 The Provider must use reasonable endeavours to complete the number of Verifications during \nthe Surge Period, as required by the demand for those services, up to the Service Cap. \n6. \nGovernance and Decision-Making Framework \nNote to Tenderers: Proposed charges for Governance and Decision-Making requirements should \nbe set out in the Attachment C (Pricing Schedule) at: \n? \nWorksheet 2: if associated with an identified deliverable; \n? \nWorksheet 3: for one-off, upfront charges as required during the Establishment Stage; or \n? \nWorksheet 4: for fixed, ongoing charges, invoiceable monthly in arrears during the life of \nthe contract \n6.1 \nThe Provider must develop and implement a Governance and Decision-Making Framework \nthat: \na. \nsets out the governance for the Methodology, including how decisions will be made and \nwho will make those decisions;  \nb. \nsets out the governance of the Application and Assessment Processes, including how \ndecisions will be made and who will make those decisions, and \nc. \nsets out the governance of the organisation, and identifies any senior roles that may \ninfluence, inform or change decisions in relation to the Methodology or decision-making \nprocess.  \n6.2 \nIn delivering the Services, the Provider must be able to continuously demonstrate: \na. \nthat First Nations people have a determinative role in the governance of the \nMethodology; \nb. \nthat First Nations people have a determinative role in the decision-making process, \nincluding conducting the three-part test; \nc. \nregular engagement with the Indigenous business sector; and \nd. \ntransparency in decision-making processes. \n6.3 \nIn delivering the Services, the Provider must: \n\n13 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \na. \nestablish and maintain a formal, Indigenous-controlled governance mechanism (e.g. \nboard or council), which oversees: \n(i) decision making on matters relating to the Methodology; and \n(ii) oversight of Application, Assessment and decision-making processes, compliance, \nand assurance processes and complaint handling processes; \nor \nb. \nensure that First Nations people are represented in the Provider?s organisational \nleadership, including in positions that: \n(i) influence or determine organisational strategy; \n(ii) control resources, budgets or workforce decisions; and  \n(iii) direct or oversee delivery of the Services. \n6.4 \nThe Provider must seek and obtain the NIAA?s written approval on the Governance and \nDecision-Making Framework before accepting Applications for Verification. \n7. \nCompliance and Assurance Framework \nNote to Tenderers: Proposed charges for Compliance and Assurance Activities should be set out \nin the Attachment C (Pricing Schedule) at: \n? \nWorksheet 2: if associated with an identified deliverable; \n? \nWorksheet 3: for one-off, upfront charges as required during the Establishment Stage; or \n? \nWorksheet 8: for fixed and variable, ongoing charges, invoiceable monthly in arrears during \nthe life of the contract. \n7.1 \nThe Provider acknowledges: \na. \nits role in ensuring the integrity of the Verifications and the information provided in the \nRegistry and its responsibility providing, independent assessments of whether an entity \n(enterprise or joint venture) is Indigenous; \nb. \nits joint responsibility with the NIAA to maintain the public?s confidence in the IPP; and \nc. \nthat it will collaborate with the NIAA to establish robust compliance and assurance \npolicies and processes.  \n7.2 \nThe Provider must develop and implement a Compliance and Assurance Framework that: \na. \nsets out how the Provider will monitor for potential non-compliance, which must include \nprocesses (Compliance Activities) for: \n(i) receiving and assessing information in real time from Verified Entities through the \nRegistry; \n(ii) receiving and assessing information in real time (at least daily) from publicly \naccessible data sources (External Data Monitoring, refer Clause 7.7 - 7.11); and \n(iii) receiving and assessing information from third party sources (e.g. complaints, media);  \nb. \nadopts a risk-based approach, which includes: \n(i) enhanced monitoring for high risk Verified Entities; and \n(ii) enhanced monitoring of Indigenous Joint Ventures and Complex Business Structures; \nc. \nsets out Compliance Outcomes, for example: \n(i) remediation; \n(ii) re-verification; \n(iii) suspension; \n(iv) deregistration; \n\n14 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n(v) investigation; \n(vi) enhanced monitoring; and \n(vii) no further action; \nd. \nsets out policies and processes, including: \n(i) key decision points and delegates; \n(ii) the escalation pathway from the identification of potential non-compliance through to \nCompliance Outcomes;  \n(iii) Verified Entities? rights and responsibilities with respect to the Compliance Activities; \nand \n(iv) clear referral pathways to law enforcement or regulatory enforcement agencies (refer \nClause 7.5 - 7.6); \ne. \nsets out Compliance Activities, including: \n(i) External Data Monitoring \n(ii) Verified Entities? Reporting Obligations; and \nf. \nsets out Assurance Activities, including: \n(i) Annual Audits; \n(ii) Indigenous Economic Benefit Reviews; and \n(iii) Spot Checks.  \n7.3 \nThe Provider must seek and obtain the NIAA?s written approval of the Compliance and \nAssurance Framework before accepting applications for Verifications.  \n7.4 \nThe Provider must report to the NIAA on Compliance and Assurance Activities as specified \nunder Data and Reports (refer Appendix 3). \nReferral to law enforcement and regulatory agencies \n7.5 \nThe Provider acknowledges: \na. \nthe risk of an Applicant or Verified Entity providing false or misleading information, \nespecially with respect to Indigeneity, in order to obtain or maintain Verification and \nensuing access to the benefits of the IPP;  \nb. \nthat by providing false or misleading information in Australian Government procurement a \nVerified Entity may be committing fraud and may face criminal penalties; and \nc. \nin delivering the Services, the Provider may receive information that indicates the \nconduct of other illicit or unethical behaviour (e.g. coercive directorships). \n7.6 \nIf the Provider has reason to believe that an Applicant or Verified Entity has breached a \nCommonwealth, state or territory law in relation to an Application or the use of, or interaction \nwith, the Registry, the Provider must notify the responsible law enforcement or regulatory \nagency of the potential breach of law.  \nCompliance: External Data Monitoring \n7.7 \nThe Provider must develop and implement a process to access publicly available data sets to \nidentify changes in Verified Entities? status, including ownership and control (External Data \nMonitoring). \n7.8 \nExternal Data Monitoring must be undertaken at least once per Business Day.  \n7.9 \nThe publicly available data sets to be accessed must include: \na. \nASIC Registers, maintained by the Australian Securities and Investments Commission;  \nb. \nthe ABR, maintained by the Australian Business Registry Services; and \n\n15 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nc. \nthe Public Register, maintained by the Office of the Registrar of Indigenous Corporations \n(ORIC). \n7.10 The Provider must access other publicly available data sets that the NIAA reasonably requests \nthe Provider to access. \n7.11 External Data Monitoring must include an escalation trigger to further Compliance Activities if it \nidentifies a change that potentially impacts a Verified Entity?s eligibility under the IPP.  \nCompliance: Verified Entities? Reporting Obligations \n7.12 The Provider must, as a condition of Verification, require Verified Entities to: \na. \nreview information held on its Record on the Registry at least once every 12 months;  \nb. \ninform the Provider within 5 Business Days of material changes, including: \n(i) changes to ownership; \n(ii) changes to control;  \n(iii) changes to its small and medium enterprise (SME) status and Australian business \nstatus; and  \nc. \nprovide any other information, or take any other action required that is reasonable to \nmaintain the accuracy and currency of the information held on their Record.  \n7.13 The Provider must, as a condition of Verification, require Verified Indigenous Joint \nVentures/Complex Business Structures to submit evidence of Indigenous Economic Benefit \n(refer Clause 7.20 onward) once every 12 months.  \n7.14 The Provider must monitor for and identify non-compliance with the requirements at Clauses \n7.12 and 7.13. \n7.15 The Provider must provide notice to Applicants and Verified Entities of the Verified Entities? \nReporting Obligations. \n7.16 The Provider must use reasonable endeavours to ensure Verified Entities regularly update or \nremove out-dated information with respect to: \n(i) the goods supplies and/or services provided; \n(ii) the locations they can supply goods and/or provide services; and \n(iii) information on capability, including licenses, certificates and compliance. \n7.17 NOT APPLICABLE \nAssurance: Annual Audits \n7.18 Beginning 12 months after the date Verifications commence, the Provider must re-assess each \nVerified Entity?s eligibility under the IPP using the Methodology (Annual Audit). \n7.19 For each Verified Entity, the first Annual Audit must be undertaken on the 12-month anniversary \nof Verification, and once every 12 months thereafter. \n7.20 The Annual Audit must consider any changes identified through the processes identified above \n(External Data Monitoring, Verified Entities reporting obligations). \nAssurance: Indigenous Economic Benefit Review \n7.21 Beginning 12 months after the date Verifications commence, the Provider must conduct an \nIndigenous Economic Benefit Review for each Indigenous Joint Venture / Complex Business \nStructure (refer Appendix 10) \n7.22 The Indigenous Economic Benefit Review:  \na. \nmust be undertaken within 12 months of the date of Verification and once every 12 \nmonths after; and \nb. \nis in addition the Annual Audit requirement. \n\n16 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n7.23 In conducting the Indigenous Economic Benefit Review, the Provider must: \na. \nrequire Indigenous Joint Business Ventures / Complex Business Structures provide \nevidence each year of Indigenous Economic Benefit of that Venture/Structure in a form to \nbe agreed between the NIAA and the Provider; and \nb. \nAssess this evidence against the Methodology.  \nAssurance: Spot checks \n7.24 Beginning 12 months after the date Verifications commence, the Provider must develop and \nimplement a process to randomly select and quality assure Verifications to: \na. \nconfirm that the Verification has correctly applied the Methodology; \nb. \nconfirm that the Assessment and Decision-making processes are defensible, free from \nbias and have appropriately considered risks; and \nc. \nidentify opportunities for improvement.  \n7.25 The Provider must undertake spot checks of 10 per cent of Verifications undertaken each year. \n7.26 The number of spot checks of Indigenous Joint Ventures / Complex Business Structures should \nbe proportionate of the total number of Indigenous Joint Ventures / Complex Business \nStructures. \nAssurance: Services and Charges \n7.27 The following Assurance Activities will commence on the 12-month anniversary of the date \nVerifications commence: \na. \nAnnual Audits; \nb. \nIndigenous Economic Benefits Review; and \nc. \nSpot Checks. \n7.28 For Indigenous Enterprises, the NIAA will pay the Provider each month the sum of two \ncomponents: \na. \nA fixed charge (Base Rate) to complete a fixed number of Assurance Activities (Base \nAmount), comprising of:  \n(i) Annual Audit (Base Amount = 500 per month); and  \n(ii) Spot Check (Base Amount = 50 per month) \nb. \nA variable charge (Additional Charge) for any additional Assurance Activities completed \nin excess of the Base Amount in the preceding month up to the Service Cap for \nAssurance Activities (refer Clauses 7.30-7.34).  \n7.29 For Indigenous Joint Ventures and Complex Business Ventures, the NIAA will pay the Provider \neach month the sum of two components: \na. \nA fixed charge (Base Rate) to complete a fixed number of Assurance Activities (Base \nAmount), comprising of:  \n(i) Annual Audit (Base Amount = 10 per month); and  \n(ii) Indigenous Economic Benefit Review (Base Amount = 10 per month); \n(iii) Spot Check (Base Amount = 1 per month). \nb. \nA variable charge (Additional Charge) for any additional Assurance Activities completed \nin excess of the Base Amount in the preceding month, up to the Service Cap for \nAssurance Activities (refer Clauses 7.30-7.34).  \nService Cap ? Assurance Activities \n7.30 The maximum number of Assurance Activities that can be completed within a month will be \nlimited to a Service Cap. \n\n17 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n7.31 The NIAA will not pay for Assurance Activities above the Service Cap unless the Provider has \nsought and obtained NIAA?s previous approval to increase the Service Cap. \n7.32 Commencing from Year 2, the Service Cap for Assurance Activities will be: \na. \nfor Verified Indigenous Enterprises: \n(i) 650 per month for Annual Audits; and \n(ii) 65 per month for Spot Checks. \nb. \nfor Verified Indigenous Joint Ventures and Complex Business Structures \n(i) 17 per month for Annual Audits; \n(ii) 17 per month for Indigenous Economic Benefit Reviews; and \n(iii) 2 per month for Spot Checks. \n7.33 The NIAA and the Provider will agree the Service Cap for Assurance Activities thereafter on the \nbasis that: \na. \nit is to be reviewed at least once every 12 months; and \nb. \nit allows the Provider reasonable opportunity to complete the number of Assurance \nActivities required (based on the total number of Verified Entities) over 12 months. \n7.34 The Provider and the NIAA may agree to change the Service Cap in writing. The NIAA will not \nunreasonably refuse a request from the Provider to increase the Service Cap. \n8. \nComplaint Handling Framework \n8.1 \nThe Provider must develop a Complaint Handling Framework that: \na. \nis consistent with the Commonwealth Ombudsman's Better Practice Complaint Handling \nGuide, the Commonwealth's model litigant obligation and administrative fairness \nprinciples; \nb. \nsets out clear referral pathways to Compliance Activities and Compliance Outcomes \n(refer Clause 7.2(c)); and \nc. \nsets out clear referral pathways to law enforcement or regulatory agencies (refer Clause \n7.6). \n8.2 \nThe Provider must: \na. \npublish the Complaint Handling Framework on the Website; and \nb. \nreview and update the Complaint Handling Framework annually. \n8.3 \nThe Provider must respond to complaints received in relation to delivery of the Services in \naccordance with the Complaint Handling Framework and Performance Expectations. \n8.4 \nThe Provider must seek and obtain the NIAA?s written approval of the Complaint Handling \nFramework before accepting Applications for Verification.  \n8.5 \nThe Provider must report to the NIAA on complaints as set out under Data and reports (refer \nAppendix 3). \n \nSolution (Registry, Website and IT) \nNote to Tenderers: Proposed charges for Registry, Website and IT requirements should be set out \nin the Attachment C (Pricing Schedule) at: \n? \nWorksheet 2: if associated with an identified deliverable;  \n? \nWorksheet 3: or one-off, upfront charges as required during the Establishment Stage; or \n? \nWorksheet 5: for fixed, ongoing charges, invoiceable monthly in arrears during the life of \nthe contract. \n\n18 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n9. \nRegistry \n9.1 \nThe Provider must develop and implement an online Registry that is accessible by Users \n(which includes the public and Commonwealth Officials). \n9.2 \nThe Registry must contain and display a record for each Verified Entity. \n9.3 \nEach Record must contain a profile of the Verified Entity with the following information: \na. \nthe registered business name and trading name; \nb. \nthe business location, which is either the main business location associated to its \nAustralian Business Number (ABN) or physical location of the premises from which it \noperates; \nc. \nprimary contact details, including telephone number, email address and postal address; \nd. \na link to its website; \ne. \nthe goods supplied and/or services provided; \nf. \nthe United Nations Standard Product and Services Code (UNSPSC) categories that \napply to the goods and/or services offered; \ng. \nthe locations it can supply goods and/or provide services; \nh. \ninformation on capability, including licenses, certificates and compliance; \ni. \nthe SME status and Australian business status as defined in Appendix B of the \nCommonwealth Procurement Rules (CPRs);  \nj. \ninformation on its recent procurement performance, including data on contracts won; \nk. \nthe date it was first registered; \nl. \nthe date the record was last updated; and \nm. \nthe date of the most recent audit.  \n9.4 \nThe Registry must include functionality to enable, encourage and monitor Verified Entities? \ncompliance against the Verified Entities? Reporting Obligations (refer Clause 7.12 ? 7.16) \nincluding: \n(i) functionality for Verified Entities to review, update and provide information required; \n(ii) issuing reminders; \n(iii) monitoring compliance with Verified Entities? Reporting Obligations; and \n(iv) identifying and reporting non-compliance on the Registry.  \n9.5 \nThe Registry must include functionality to enable users to: \na. \nsearch, and refine searches by key terms to be agreed with the NIAA and including: \n(i) Business name; \n(ii) Trading name; \n(iii) ABN; \n(iv) goods supplied and/or services provided; \n(v) business location; and  \n(vi) location goods can be supplied and/or services provided; and \nb. \nsave, download or export search results in common spreadsheet formats (files with \nextensions .xlsx, xlsm, .xml or csv.) or in formats agreed with the NIAA.  \n9.6 \nThe Provider must make reasonable efforts to ensure that information published on the \nRegistry is accurate and up-to-date.  \n9.7 \nThe Registry must include functionality to enable: \na. \na record to be updated within 3 Business Days of being notified of a change; \n\n19 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nb. \npermanent removal of the relevant Record from the Registry if a Verified Entity does not \nmeet the strengthened IPP eligibility criteria (Deregistration); \nc. \ntemporary removal of the relevant Record from the Registry if there is reasonable \nsuspicion that a Verified Entity does not meet the strengthened IPP eligibility criteria \n(Suspension); and \nd. \ntemporary removal of the relevant Record from the Registry if the Verified Entity does not \ncomply with Verified Entities? Reporting Obligations. \n9.8 \nUnless agreed otherwise in writing with the NIAA, the Provider must facilitate two rounds of \nuser testing with NIAA and Australian Government procurement officials before the Registry is \nmade publicly available. \n9.9 \nThe Registry must meet or comply with: \na. \nAustralian Government Digital Service Standards, including in relation to Information \nArchitecture, content usage and accessibility, readability, responsive design, create once, \npublish everywhere, Image optimisation, and continuous improvement;3 \nb. \nuser testing as set out in the Service Level Standards at Appendix 5; and  \nc. \nother standards as agreed by the Provider and the NIAA in writing from time to time.  \n9.10 The Registry must meet, comply with or exceed the Performance Expectations as identified in \nAppendix 6, in regard to: \n(i) Registry availability; \n(ii) Registry update time; \n(iii) Registry search response time; and  \n(iv) other Performance Expectations agreed by the Provider and the NIAA as set out in \nAppendix 5.  \n9.11 The Registry must comply with: \na. \nall relevant Laws; \nb. \napplicable policies; \nc. \nthe Protective Security Policy Framework; and \nd. \nthe Australian Privacy Principles. \n9.12 The Provider must report to the NIAA on the Registry as specified under Data and Reports \n(refer Appendix 3). \n10. \nWebsite \n10.1 The Provider must develop, implement and maintain a publicly accessible website (Website). \n10.2 The Provider is responsible for: \na. \nweb hosting; \nb. \ncontent and design; \nc. \nsecurity; \nd. \nApplication updates; \ne. \nbusiness continuity, disaster recovery and backups; and \nf. \ncosts associated with the above.  \n10.3 The Website must include and make publicly accessible: \n \n3 See: Digital Service Standards - https://www.digital.gov.au/policy/digital-experience/digital-service-standard; and \nAccessibility obligations (WCAG and government accessibility expectations), https://www.dta.gov.au/accessibility. \n\n20 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \na. \na home page; \nb. \ninformation about the Provider, including contact details;  \nc. \ndetails of planned consultation; \nd. \nfacilities allowing for the lodgement of Expressions of Interest (EOI) during the Interim \nPeriod; \ne. \ngeneral information, on: \n(i) the IPP; \n(ii) the NIAA?s role; \n(iii) advice that the Services are funded by the NIAA under the Indigenous Advancement \nStrategy (IAS); and  \nf. \nhyperlinks to externally hosted resources that are relevant to the Australian Government \nprocurement context, Indigenous procurement and/or Indigenous economic development \nopportunities; \ng. \nthe Registry (including search functions); \nh. \nGuidance (refer Clause 12.2 - 12.3); and  \ni. \nfacilities allowing for the lodgement of: \n(i) enquiry;  \n(ii) a complaint; and \n(iii) Data and Reports as agreed with the NIAA. \n10.4 The Website must include Authenticated Pages that are accessible only through registration \nand login for interested entities, Applicants and Verified Entities to facilitate the submission of: \na. \nApplications, including functionality to upload supporting documents; \nb. \nvoluntary updates; and  \nc. \nVerified Entities? Reporting Obligations. \n10.5 Unless agreed otherwise with the NIAA in writing, the Website and domain must operate \nindependently from any other website or domain, including any website or domain the Provider \noperates for other purposes.  \n10.6 Access to the Website, including to the Registry and other resources accessible from it, must \nbe free to users at all times. \n10.7 The Website must meet or comply with: \na. \nthe Australian Government Digital Service Standard including in relation to Information \nArchitecture, content usage and accessibility, readability, responsive design, create once, \npublish everywhere, Image optimisation, and continuous improvement;4 and \nb. \nother standards agreed by the Provider and the NIAA in writing from time to time.  \n10.8 The Website must meet, comply with or exceed Performance Expectations as identified in \nAppendix 6, in regard to: \na. \nWebsite availability \nb. \nuser testing; \nc. \nweb page load time; and \nd. \nother Performance Expectations agreed by the Provider and the NIAA. \n \n4 See: Digital Service Standards - https://www.digital.gov.au/policy/digital-experience/digital-service-standard; and \nAccessibility obligations (WCAG and government accessibility expectations) - \nhttps://www.dta.gov.au/accessibility. \n\n21 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n10.9 Unless agreed otherwise with the NIAA in writing, the Provider must: \na. \nfacilitate two rounds of user testing with Australian Government procurement officials \nbefore the Website is publicly available; and \nb. \npass the final round of user testing with at least 90% acceptance rate. \n10.10 Unless agreed otherwise with the NIAA in writing, the Provider must shut down the Website as \nset out in the Contract. \n10.11 The Provider must report to the NIAA on the Registry as specified under Data and Reports \n(refer Appendix 3). \n11. \nIT requirements \n11.1 The Provider must develop, implement and maintain IT infrastructure to: \na. \nreceive Expressions of Interest (refer Clause 14.14-14.15); \nb. \nreceive and process enquiries (refer Clause 12.1); \nc. \nreceive and process Applications (refer Clause 5.12-5.14); \nd. \nreceive and process updates from Verified Entities (refer Clause 9.4); \ne. \nreceive and process complaints (including supporting documents); \nf. \nreceive and assess information in real time (at least daily) from publicly accessible data \nsources (External Data Monitoring, refer Clause 7.7 ? 7.11); \ng. \nundertake Data Integration (refer Clause 11.5 ? 11.6); and \nh. \ngenerate Data and Reports (refer Clause 20). \n11.2 The Provider must  \na. \nseek and obtain prior written approval from the NIAA if it intends to incorporate the use of \nArtificial Intelligence (AI) technologies to deliver any component of the Services; and  \nb. \ncomply with the following Standards: \ni. \nAS ISO/IEC 42001:2023 Information technology - Artificial intelligence - \nManagement system; \nii. \nAS ISO/IEC 23894:2023 Information technology - Artificial intelligence - Guidance \non risk management; \niii. \nAS ISO/IEC 38507:2022 Information technology - Governance of IT - Governance \nimplications of the use of artificial intelligence by organizations;  \niv. \nthe AI guardrails set out in the Voluntary AI Safety Standard and any successor \nmandatory standards.5 \n11.3 The Provider must not use AI to conduct Verifications or application of the three-part test. \n11.4 The Provider must meet with the NIAA in accordance with the schedule for Contract \ngovernance and meetings (refer Appendix 4) to resolve implementation issues and review \npolicy settings. \nData Integration \n11.5 The Provider must deliver Application Program Interface (API) functionality for up to 10 \nCommonwealth systems to enable integration (live or overnight) of publicly available data from \nthe Registry.  \n11.6 At launch of the Registry, there must be (at a minimum) 2 APIs, comprising: \n \n5 The Voluntary AI Safety Standard was issued by the Department of Industry, Science and Resources in August \n2024 and updated in December 2025. More information is available here: \nhttps://www.industry.gov.au/publications/voluntary-ai-safety-standard \n\n22 \n \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \na. \n1 x API endpoint for the IPP Reporting Solution (the NIAA); and  \nb. \n1 x API endpoint for the Department of Finance (AusTender, GovPanels, and Supplier \nPortal). \nCybersecurity: \n11.7 The Provider must: \na. \ncomply with the Protective Security Policy Framework Policy 6 (Third party risk \nmanagement) and Policy 10 (Information Holdings);6 \nb. \ncomply with the Australian Signals Directorate (ASD) Information Security Manual (ISM). \nThis includes implementing risk appropriate ISM controls for internet facing services; web \napplication hardening; patch management; logging and monitoring;7 \nc. \ncomply with the ASD Essential 8 compliance Maintain Essential Eight Maturity Level 1, \nwith a preference for Level 2 for Patching, applications and operating systems; multi \nfactor authentication for admin access and daily backups; and  \nd. \nprovide evidence of maturity of the above-mentioned controls (self-assessment or \nindependent report). \n11.8 The Provider must: \na. \ncomply with the Laws that apply to the handling of Personally Identifiable Information \n(PII) data. This includes compliance with the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth) and the Australian \nPrivacy Principles (APPs) including: \n(i) technical and organisation security controls (APP 11); \n(ii) data minimisation and retention limits; and  \n(iii) secure disposal or de identification.  \nSpecific controls around the hosting and Solution development \n11.9 The Provider must \na. \nensure that the hosting environment is located in Australia and covered by an ISM \naligned security framework; \nb. \nimplement secure development practices including protection against Open Web \nApplication Security Project Top 10 risks; input validation, access controls, and session \nmanagement; \nc. \nincorporate the following mandatory features into the Solution: \n(i) HTTPS/TLS (not HTTP);  \n(ii) security header enforcement; and \n(iii) hardened web server configuration per ASD guidance;  \nd. \nensure that the Solution uses centralised logging for authentication events; data \nchanges; administrative actions; and logs retained and protected in line with ISM \nguidance;  \ne. \nmandatory remediati
